config MESHTASTIC_NETLOG_FILTER
	bool "Restrict noisy modules to WRN+ on the net syslog backend only"
	default y
	depends on LOG_BACKEND_NET
	depends on LOG_RUNTIME_FILTERING
	help
	  Zephyr's runtime log filtering (log_filter_set()) is genuinely
	  per-(backend, module): a message can be created at the compiled
	  ceiling (CONFIG_<MODULE>_LOG_LEVEL) but only actually SENT to a
	  given backend if that backend's own per-module filter allows it.
	  This restricts a curated list of driver/protocol-internals modules
	  to WRN-or-worse on log_backend_net specifically -- the shell/console
	  backend is untouched and keeps seeing whatever each module is
	  compiled at.

	  Exists because CONFIG_LOG_BACKEND_NET has repeatedly caused real
	  stability problems at volume: a resolved net_pkt/net_buf exhaustion
	  bug (DEBUG-level LoRa+meshtastic logging saturated the TX pool), a
	  resolved GNSS UART-overrun flood implicated in household WiFi
	  interference (the bench WiFi shares a radio with the house WiFi),
	  and a heap-alloc trace that lost ~85% of its own output to the same
	  flood class. All three were DIFFERENT modules getting loud at
	  DIFFERENT times -- this exists to hold a standing, curated ceiling
	  on the modules most likely to do that again, rather than reactively
	  chasing the next one.

	  Deliberately does NOT touch modules the bench's own passive netlog
	  tooling depends on (meshtastic, meshtastic_sample, meshtastic_sntp,
	  thread_analyzer) -- see meshtastic_netlog_filter.c for the exact
	  list and the reasoning per module, including meshtastic_powermon,
	  which is a real judgment call (fires every RX/TX state transition;
	  restricted here since it's real volume, but it was also this
	  session's main "is this node radio-active" signal -- easy to move
	  back to the keep list if that visibility is missed more than the
	  bandwidth is).
